WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.460
We were promised a non-terrifying hedgehog,
and by gosh, it looks like we'll get one.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:11.040
On October 26th, a Sonic fan account on Twitter
posted a picture of a standee for the upcoming

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.450
film Sonic the Hedgehog that appears to confirm
that recently leaked images of the title character's

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.400
redesigned look were genuine.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.060
Assuming that the new photo of the standee
isn't doctored, it sure looks like somebody

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.670
really did have a friend at Paramount who
managed to smuggle out artwork featuring Sonic's

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:28.500
new design.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.580
In contrast to the version of the speedy blue
hedgehog featured in the movie's first trailer,

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:38.380
Sonic now looks much, much more video game-y,
and much less like the result of some terrifyingly

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.420
unethical genetics experiment.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:45.180
As you can see, the look is classic Sonic,
from the shape of his eyes to his gloved hands

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.520
to his body, which is far less humanoid.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.480
This lines right up with the two images that
were previously leaked, one of which appears

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:56.421
to actually be the same image used on the
standee, and the other which shows the character

00:00:56.421 --> 00:00:57.260
from the front.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.200
Another thing the images have in common?

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.960
Neither of them seem likely to give you nightmares,
like the old design did.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.680
The flick's director, Jeff Fowler, and his
VFX team going back to the drawing board like

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.100
this is unusual.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.380
It's the only time we can think of that massive
fan outcry in response to a film's promotional

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.380
materials was acknowledged and the issue corrected.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.259
Fowler and his crew even pushed back the movie's
initial release date of November 8 by several

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.800
months to allow them time to properly dial
in Sonic's look.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.280
Basically, the team responsible for Sonic's
design got just about everything wrong that

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.980
they possibly could have gotten wrong the
first time around.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.380
Those weird-looking, almost human eyes look
nothing like what we're used to from Sonic,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.109
but they're totally spot-on when compared
to the teeth, which simply appear to be a

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:43.500
set of actual human teeth, for some mysterious
reason.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.100
Did anyone really want Sonic to have people
teeth?

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.940
"No, but thank you for asking."

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.460
The design of Sonic's body wasn't any better.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.940
Once again, the animators leaned into giving
the fuzzy little guy oddly human proportions,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.890
including hands and feet that were much smaller
than Sonic's video game look, and legs that

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.500
could only be described as "disturbingly buff."

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.760
"Gotta work out year-round, little bro.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.560
It's part of being healthy."

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.140
While the trailer made the movie itself look
pretty fun, and promised a return to gonzo

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:17.430
comic form by Jim Carrey, fans simply couldn't
get past the fact that their beloved hedgehog

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.670
looked so unfamiliar and off-putting, and
they took to Twitter in droves to let Fowler

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.520
and company know about it.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.740
To his credit, Fowler responded almost immediately,
and what he had to say went a long way toward

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.560
soothing angry Sonic fans.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.460
In a tweet, the director wrote,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.040
"Thank you for the support.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:34.940
And the criticism.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:39.560
The message is loud and clear...you aren't
happy with the design &amp; you want changes.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:40.720
It's going to happen.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.420
Everyone at Paramount &amp; Sega are fully committed
to making this character the BEST he can be."

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.860
A few weeks later, Fowler announced via Twitter
that the flick had been pushed back in order

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.100
to allow him and his team, quote, "a little
more time to make Sonic just right."

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:58.780
That was pretty much the last we'd heard of
the whole thing until those leaked images

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.770
hit the web, and it's pretty obvious that
the flick's VFX artists have been busting

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:07.160
their butts making sure that Sonic looks as
video game-accurate as possible.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.520
Fans across Twitter breathed a collective
sigh of relief in response to Sonic's new

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.650
look, and it's likely just a matter of time
before we get a new trailer featuring the

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.120
rejiggered design, and probably a winking
in-joke or two about the initial design's

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:20.900
reception.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.660
That original trailer will live on in infamy
until the heat death of the internet, though,

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.110
and will serve as a timeless example of how
not to render iconic video game characters

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:33.880
so that they look like something your child
is convinced hides under their bed every night.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:39.020
Sonic the Hedgehog comes sprinting into theaters
on February 14th, 2020.

